Attach bluetooth from userspace only where the kernel has not

hciattach is told to take /dev/ttyS1 to 2000000 baud, and the controller obeys
the vendor command and switches. The host cannot follow: the meson UART is
clocked at 24 MHz, so base_baud is 24000000/16 = 1500000 and 2 Mbaud is above
what it can generate. The two ends then run at different rates, which leaves
short exchanges working while sustained traffic arrives corrupt. Take the
ceiling from the port itself, so a UART clocked high enough still runs at
2000000.

Stand aside entirely on a board whose device tree gives the port a bluetooth
child: hci_uart has bound the controller before this runs, and a second attach
would fight it for the same tty.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/projects/Amlogic-ce/packages/linux-firmware/brcmfmac_sdio-firmware-aml/system.d/brcmfmac_sdio-firmware-aml.service
+++ b/projects/Amlogic-ce/packages/linux-firmware/brcmfmac_sdio-firmware-aml/system.d/brcmfmac_sdio-firmware-aml.service
@@ -3,9 +3,12 @@
 Wants=bluetooth.service
 Before=bluetooth.service
 After=dev-ttyS1.device
+# Where the device tree gives the port a bluetooth child, hci_uart has the
+# controller already; attaching again from userspace fights it for the tty.
+ConditionPathExistsGlob=!/sys/class/bluetooth/hci*
 
 [Service]
 Type=simple
 ExecStartPre=/usr/sbin/rfkill unblock bluetooth
-ExecStart=/usr/bin/hciattach -n -s 115200 /dev/ttyS1 bcm43xx 2000000
+ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'clk=$$(cat /sys/class/tty/ttyS1/uartclk 2>/dev/null || echo 0); max=$$((clk / 16)); rate=2000000; [ "$$max" -gt 0 ] && [ "$$max" -lt "$$rate" ] && rate=$$max; exec /usr/bin/hciattach -n -s 115200 /dev/ttyS1 bcm43xx "$$rate"'
 ExecStopPost=/usr/sbin/rfkill block bluetooth
